{:edb/db-before-t 2166 :edb/db-after-t 2167 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535479 50 #inst "2026-10-09T00:57:00.174Z" 13194139535479 true] [17592186124598 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351283 13194139535479 true] [17592186124598 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351290 13194139535479 true] [17592186124598 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351297 13194139535479 true] [17592186124599 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351304 13194139535479 true] [17592186124599 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351321 13194139535479 true] [17592186351283 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351284 13194139535479 true] [17592186351283 1089 "q-342306" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351283 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535479 true] [17592186351283 1100 "$$\n({x}^{2}-4){y}^{″}+5x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}-4}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{x}^{2}-4}$ are analytic about $x=2.$\n- The functions $(x-2)P(x)$ and $(x-2)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=2.$\n- The point $x=2$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation." 13194139535479 true] [17592186351283 1113 #uuid "3b6f611b-31ec-5ad6-9698-7aba48a1a8d3" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351283 1145 "A singular point $x=a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $({x}^{2}-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-4){y}^{″}+5x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}-4}{y}^{′}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{x}^{2}-4}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{x}^{2}-4}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=2,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=2.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x-2)P(x)=(x-2)\\cdot \\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}-4}=\\frac{5x}{x+2}\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=2,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}Q(x)=(x-2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{x}^{2}-4}=\\frac{(x-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{x+2}\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=2.$ Therefore, statement II is true.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=2$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is true, it follows that $x=2$ is a **regular** singular point. Thus, statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351284 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351285 13194139535479 true] [17592186351284 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351286 13194139535479 true] [17592186351284 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351287 13194139535479 true] [17592186351284 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351288 13194139535479 true] [17592186351284 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351289 13194139535479 true] [17592186351284 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535479 true] [17592186351284 1041 "selection" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351284 1104 #edb/ref 17592186351289 13194139535479 true] [17592186351284 1110 #uuid "be70a0a2-59de-5ed2-9592-1060541ce22d" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351285 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351285 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535479 true] [17592186351285 1111 #uuid "70ba6c2a-317c-56b1-b222-126ef8d9f4c1" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351286 1034 "I only" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351286 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535479 true] [17592186351286 1111 #uuid "9949a42c-0454-5df9-8356-e8ca35314590" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351287 1034 "III only" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351287 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535479 true] [17592186351287 1111 #uuid "146cc169-3452-5061-82b6-8547736796d7" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351288 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351288 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535479 true] [17592186351288 1111 #uuid "b1840283-b098-56a3-9f95-cfe960bbab8f" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351289 1034 "II  only" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351289 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535479 true] [17592186351289 1111 #uuid "89f62aba-ad98-5602-902b-1afbcc9f0a46" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351290 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351291 13194139535479 true] [17592186351290 1089 "q-342307" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351290 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535479 true] [17592186351290 1100 "$$\n(x+2){y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x+2}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}$ are analytic about $x=-2.$\n- The functions $(x+2)P(x)$ and $(x+2)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=-2.$\n- The point $x=-2$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation." 13194139535479 true] [17592186351290 1113 #uuid "a478395d-5888-5691-9b70-b1f926c9a6c2" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351290 1145 "A singular point $a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x+2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2){y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x+2}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=-2,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=-2.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x+2)P(x)=(x+2)\\frac{1}{x+2}=1\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-2,$ while the function\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{2}Q(x)=(x+2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{x+2}\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=-2$ since its denominator vanishes at $x=-2.$ Therefore, statement II is false.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=-2$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is false, it follows that $x=-2$ is an **irregular** singular point. Thus, statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351291 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351292 13194139535479 true] [17592186351291 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351293 13194139535479 true] [17592186351291 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351294 13194139535479 true] [17592186351291 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351295 13194139535479 true] [17592186351291 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351296 13194139535479 true] [17592186351291 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535479 true] [17592186351291 1041 "selection" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351291 1104 #edb/ref 17592186351293 13194139535479 true] [17592186351291 1110 #uuid "aed75b49-d600-5c51-adee-4f42f69349b0" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351292 1034 "I only" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351292 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535479 true] [17592186351292 1111 #uuid "8ea1e8c9-77a2-58dd-b914-87e05eaf1a0a" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351293 1034 "III only" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351293 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535479 true] [17592186351293 1111 #uuid "1f12d821-901a-5a55-8410-84eb18684485" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351294 1034 "None" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351294 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535479 true] [17592186351294 1111 #uuid "ff2c4ad0-e7a4-54c0-aec3-16f7c37daad4" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351295 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351295 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535479 true] [17592186351295 1111 #uuid "621efa39-45b1-5d37-bfe4-751f242d2a59" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351296 1034 "II only" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351296 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535479 true] [17592186351296 1111 #uuid "93499243-4600-582e-8f7e-f01cf3f41a36" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351297 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351298 13194139535479 true] [17592186351297 1089 "q-342369" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351297 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535479 true] [17592186351297 1100 "$$\n(x-4){y}^{″}+\\frac{2}{x-4}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{2}{(x-4)^{2}}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{3}}$ are analytic about $x=4.$\n- The functions $(x-4)P(x)$ and $(x-4)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=4.$\n- The point $x=4$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation." 13194139535479 true] [17592186351297 1113 #uuid "1a5a8137-5abd-59c8-8d57-52f5cffa1a58" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351297 1145 "A singular point $a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4){y}^{″}+\\frac{2}{x-4}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{2}{(x-4)^{2}}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{3}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{2}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=4,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=4.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x-4)P(x)=(x-4)\\cdot \\frac{2}{(x-4)^{2}}=\\frac{2}{x-4}\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=4,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-4)^{2}Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x-4}\n$$\n\nis also *not* analytic about $x=4$ since its denominator vanishes at $x=4.$ Therefore, statement II is false.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=4$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is false, it follows that $x=4$ is an **irregular** singular point. Thus, statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351298 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351299 13194139535479 true] [17592186351298 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351300 13194139535479 true] [17592186351298 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351301 13194139535479 true] [17592186351298 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351302 13194139535479 true] [17592186351298 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351303 13194139535479 true] [17592186351298 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535479 true] [17592186351298 1041 "selection" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351298 1104 #edb/ref 17592186351300 13194139535479 true] [17592186351298 1110 #uuid "d32288de-24b1-51e4-92e9-3d727d2a0769" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351299 1034 "I only" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351299 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535479 true] [17592186351299 1111 #uuid "80768076-c326-5655-8f02-657706f102ab" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351300 1034 "III only" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351300 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535479 true] [17592186351300 1111 #uuid "836ec4a8-77f0-5bd8-acee-99b1169eca01" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351301 1034 "None" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351301 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535479 true] [17592186351301 1111 #uuid "96bb5f65-c79e-5a0a-9adc-4c2d7d677226" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351302 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351302 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535479 true] [17592186351302 1111 #uuid "800afd5e-ef98-52f7-b995-af257074338d" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351303 1034 "II only" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351303 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535479 true] [17592186351303 1111 #uuid "99cc7404-a637-54dc-b826-4c6242067085" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351304 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351305 13194139535479 true] [17592186351304 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351309 13194139535479 true] [17592186351304 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351313 13194139535479 true] [17592186351304 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351317 13194139535479 true] [17592186351304 1089 "q-342416" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351304 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535479 true] [17592186351304 1100 "$$\nx(x-1)(x+3){y}^{″}+(x+3){y}^{′}+x(x-1)y=0\n$$\n\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.$\\,$The point $x=2$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=-3$ is\n{{field-2}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=0$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=1$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation." 13194139535479 true] [17592186351304 1113 #uuid "fea7ff9b-65a7-5f89-87f2-04b4e9266d72" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351304 1145 "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $x(x-1)(x+3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x-1)(x+3){y}^{″}+(x+3){y}^{′}+x(x-1)y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x(x-1)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x+3}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x(x-1)}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x+3}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x(x-1)=0,$ which occurs at $x=0$ and $x=1.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x+3=0,$ which occurs at $x=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is $\\text{an ordinary}$ point of the differential equation, and the singular points are\n\n$$\nx=-3,\\,x=0,\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=-3,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+3)P(x)=(x+3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x-1)},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-3,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x+3)^{2}Q(x)=(x+3)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+3}=x+3,\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=-3.$ Therefore, $x=-3$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=0,$ the function\n\n$$\nxP(x)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x-1)}=\\frac{1}{x-1},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=0,$ and the function\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}Q(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+3},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=0.$ Therefore, $x=0$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=1,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-1)P(x)=(x-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x-1)}=\\frac{1}{x},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=1,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}Q(x)=(x-1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+3},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=1.$ Therefore, $x=1$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation." 13194139535479 true] [17592186351305 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351306 13194139535479 true] [17592186351305 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351307 13194139535479 true] [17592186351305 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351308 13194139535479 true] [17592186351305 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535479 true] [17592186351305 1041 "field-1" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351305 1104 #edb/ref 17592186351307 13194139535479 true] [17592186351305 1110 #uuid "d69d56ab-4ee0-5a15-9d3b-5efcf681ef96" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351306 1034 "an irregular singular" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351306 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535479 true] [17592186351306 1111 #uuid "bf3f2b28-c51a-5833-979a-907f69cdc014" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351307 1034 "an ordinary" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351307 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535479 true] [17592186351307 1111 #uuid "0e516e93-231b-5896-8e79-db7362f326f1" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351308 1034 "a regular singular" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351308 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535479 true] [17592186351308 1111 #uuid "f64e6176-3081-5ff1-950e-90dac73d3aaf" 13194139535479 true] [17592186351309 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351310 13194139535479 true] [17592186351309 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351311 13194139535479 true] [17592186351309 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351312 13194139535479 true] [17592186351309 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535479 true] [17592186351309 1041 